## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of a practical low-level concurrency primitive (SPSC) and memory ordering without locks in a real data-path scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Ring buffer wrap-around and capacity checks
- SPSC invariants: single producer advances tail, single consumer advances head
- Atomic head/tail with acquire/release semantics
- Correct visibility guarantees without heavy synchronization

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for SPSC queue
typedef struct { int data; } Item;
#define CAP 1024
Item buf[CAP];
std::atomic<size_t> head(0), tail(0);

bool enqueue(Item it){
  size_t h = head.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
  size_t t = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  if (t - h >= CAP) return false; // full
  buf[t % CAP] = it;
  tail.store(t+1, std::memory_order_release);
  return true;
}

bool dequeue(Item* out){
  size_t h = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  size_t t = tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
  if (t - h == 0) return false; // empty
  *out = buf[h % CAP];
  head.store(h+1, std::memory_order_release);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers/consumers without stalls?
- What tests would you add to detect drift or data loss under bursty input?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of per-thread buffering, cache-line alignment, and atomic merges to minimize contention in high-frequency workloads.

## Key Concepts
- Thread-local buffers to avoid contention
- Cache-line alignment to prevent false sharing
- Atomic merges with memory_order_relaxed for performance

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdalign.h>
#define NBINS 256

typedef struct {
  alignas(64) unsigned long long bins[NBINS];
} thread_hist_t;

unsigned long long global_bins[NBINS];

void add_sample(thread_hist_t* t, unsigned int v) {
  t->bins[v]++;
}

void merge_all_to_global(thread_hist_t* t) {
  for (int i = 0; i < NBINS; ++i)
    __atomic_fetch_add(&global_bins[i], t->bins[i], __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  // optionally clear t->bins here
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for dynamic bin counts or streaming merges?
- What profiling steps would you take to quantify false-sharing effects and contention under multi-threaded load?

## Why This Is Asked
Low-level memory management on constrained hardware.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size allocator (slab)
- Free-list stored inside blocks; index-based chaining
- O(1) allocations/deallocations with minimal metadata
- Alignment, memory overhead, and fragmentation considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct {
  uint32_t next; // index of next free slot
} FreeSlot;

#define POOL_SIZE 256
#define SLOT_SIZE 64
static uint8_t pool[POOL_SIZE * SLOT_SIZE];
static uint32_t head = 0; // index of first free slot
static uint32_t used = 0;

void slab_init(void) {
  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < POOL_SIZE - 1; ++i) {
    ((uint32_t*)pool)[i] = i + 1;
  }
  ((uint32_t*)pool)[POOL_SIZE - 1] = UINT32_MAX;
  head = 0;
  used = 0;
}

void* slab_alloc(void) {
  if (head == UINT32_MAX) return NULL;
  uint32_t idx = head;
  head = ((uint32_t*)pool)[idx];
  ++used;
  return pool + idx * SLOT_SIZE;
}

void slab_free(void* p) {
  ptrdiff_t idx = ((uint8_t*)p - pool) / SLOT_SIZE;
  ((uint32_t*)pool)[idx] = head;
  head = (uint32_t)idx;
  --used;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect fragmentation in this design?
- How would you extend this to multi-threaded contexts with minimal locking?

## Why This Is Asked\n\nTests understanding of inter-thread synchronization without kernel primitives, and memory ordering.\\n\n## Key Concepts\n- Atomic operations, memory_order semantics\\n- Barrier synchronization, generation counters\\n- Spin-wait considerations and pause\n\n## Code Example\n```c
typedef struct {
  atomic_int count;
  atomic_uint64_t gen;
  int N;
} barrier_t;

void barrier_enter(barrier_t* b, int id) {
  uint64_t g = atomic_load_explicit(&b->gen, memory_order_relaxed);
  if (atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&b->count, 1, memory_order_acq_rel) == b->N - 1) {
    atomic_store_explicit(&b->count, 0, memory_order_relaxed);
    atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&b->gen, 1, memory_order_release);
  } else {
    while (atomic_load_explicit(&b->gen, memory_order_acquire) == g) {
      _mm_pause();
    }
  }
}
```
\n## Follow-up Questions\n- How would you ensure fairness if threads stall? \n- How would you extend for multiple, consecutive barrier phases?\n

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of basic memory management without dynamic allocation, a common pattern in low-latency systems.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size pool basics
- Free-list within block memory
- Alignment and safety checks
- Simple double-free detection (debug)

## Code Example

```c
#define BLOCK_SIZE 64
#define N_BLOCKS 1024
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; unsigned char payload[BLOCK_SIZE - sizeof(struct Block*)]; } Block;
static Block blocks[N_BLOCKS];
static Block* free_list;

void pool_init(void){
  for(int i=0;i<N_BLOCKS-1;i++) blocks[i].next = &blocks[i+1];
  blocks[N_BLOCKS-1].next = NULL;
  free_list = &blocks[0];
}
void* pool_alloc(void){
  if(!free_list) return NULL;
  Block* b = free_list;
  free_list = b->next;
  return (void*)b->payload;
}
void pool_free(void* p){
  Block* b = (Block*)((char*)p - offsetof(Block, payload));
  b->next = free_list;
  free_list = b;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect double-free in a release build?
- How would you extend this to multi-threaded use without locks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of cache-like structures, address translation, and replacement policies at low level. 

## Key Concepts
- TLB structure and page number tagging
- Linear probing and wraparound
- LRU replacement and correctness in a small cache
- Interaction with two-level page tables during misses

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint32_t tag; uint32_t pa; uint8_t valid; uint8_t lru; } tlb_entry;
static tlb_entry tlb[256];
void tlb_init() { for (int i=0;i<256;i++) tlb[i].valid=0; }
bool tlb_lookup(uint32_t va, uint32_t* pa){ uint32_t page = va >> 12; uint32_t idx = page & 0xFF; for(int i=0;i<256;i++){ tlb_entry* e = &tlb[(idx+i)&0xFF]; if(!e->valid) continue; if(e->tag==page){ *pa = e->pa; e->lru = 0; return true; } e->lru++; } return false; }
void tlb_insert(uint32_t va, uint32_t pa){ uint32_t page = va >> 12; uint32_t idx = page & 0xFF; int miss_pos = -1; uint8_t oldest = 0; for(int i=0;i<256;i++){ tlb_entry* e = &tlb[(idx+i)&0xFF]; if(!e->valid){ miss_pos = (idx+i)&0xFF; break; } if(e->lru > oldest){ oldest = e->lru; miss_pos = (idx+i)&0xFF; } }
 tlb[miss_pos].tag = page; tlb[miss_pos].pa = pa; tlb[miss_pos].valid = 1; tlb[miss_pos].lru = 0; }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to multi-core without races?
- What trade-offs arise if you instead make it a set-associative cache?
